<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
com.throne.throne-herdr.plist — throne-owned macOS LaunchAgent template for
the herdr terminal-workspace server (throne session); the mac counterpart of
throne/systemd/throne-herdr.service.

Not loadable as it stands: `{{HERDR_BIN}}` (absolute path of the herdr binary)
is substituted at install time, and only the rendered copy in the user's
LaunchAgents dir is a working agent. Install with:
  cd <throne root> && ./install.sh        (or ./bin/throne-cli install-services)

Enabled and bootstrapped by install-services, exactly like its systemd twin
is `enable --now`ed on linux. Its predecessor com.throne.herdr-server is
retired (RETIRED_DARWIN_AGENTS in src/install-services/darwin.ts): a box that
still has it loaded gets it booted out before this one is bootstrapped, so
two servers never fight for the throne session socket.

PROVEN on a real mac (2026-09-02, macOS 26 / Darwin 25.6): Apple's plist
parser accepts this file with the comment block in this position, and
`launchctl print gui/<uid>/com.throne.throne-herdr`, `launchctl enable`,
`launchctl bootstrap gui/<uid> <plist>` and `launchctl bootout` all behave
as install-services expects. Earlier revisions of this file carried a long
"no macOS host exists" disclaimer; that bound no longer applies.

KEYS SHIPPED BELOW
  Label             a launchctl service target is built from this label
                    rather than from the filename.
  ProgramArguments  /bin/bash as a login shell, so the server and every agent
                    pane it spawns inherit the PATH the .profile chain builds
                    (mise shims, brew). A launchd agent otherwise starts with
                    the bare system PATH, which reaches none of them. The
                    server's own output goes to ~/Library/Logs/throne/ —
                    there is no journal on a mac, and StandardOutPath does
                    not expand ~, so the redirect lives inside the shell.
  RunAtLoad         launchd.plist(5): "This optional key is used to control
                    whether your job is launched once at the time the job is
                    loaded. The default is false."
  KeepAlive         launchd.plist(5): "The value may be set to true to
                    unconditionally keep the job alive." It stands in for the
                    linux unit's Restart=always.
  ThrottleInterval  launchd.plist(5): "by default, jobs will not be spawned
                    more than once every 10 seconds." 10 is that documented
                    default; the linux unit's RestartSec=3 is not reproduced.

SHARP EDGE — a herdr behavior, not a launchd one: `herdr update` with its
handoff flag makes the OLD server spawn the new one as a detached daemon,
which then fights this agent for the socket. Update without the handoff flag
and restart the agent explicitly between agent runs instead:
  launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.throne.throne-herdr
Any restart drops every live pane.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.throne.throne-herdr</string>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/bash</string>
		<string>-lc</string>
		<string>mkdir -p "$HOME/Library/Logs/throne" &amp;&amp; exec {{HERDR_BIN}} --session throne server &gt;&gt; "$HOME/Library/Logs/throne/throne-herdr.log" 2&gt;&amp;1</string>
	</array>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<true/>
	<key>ThrottleInterval</key>
	<integer>10</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
